# ADR-004 — Actor

> **The canonical representation of intentional elements within the Creative Representation Layer.**

**Status:** Accepted

---

# Context

Creative works are composed of intentional elements that contribute to
meaning.

These elements may be people, animals, products, vehicles, architecture,
objects, environments, or entirely fictional creations.

Traditional scene models often classify these elements according to their
physical nature.

VizClick instead models creativity.

Within a creative representation:

- a luxury watch can become the protagonist
- a mountain can become the emotional focus
- a forest can become a character
- a crowd can become a single visual entity
- a galaxy can become an intentional design element

The architecture therefore requires an abstraction based on creative
purpose rather than physical classification.

---

# Decision

VizClick adopts **Actor** as the canonical representation of every
intentional element within the Creative Representation Layer (CRL).

An Actor is an architectural abstraction.

It represents the creative role performed by an element rather than its
physical identity.

Actors are renderer-independent.

They never describe implementation details.

Instead, they describe creative intent that can later be translated by a
Renderer Adapter.

Every intentional element within VizClick is represented as an Actor.

---

# Actor Types

Every Actor represents a single intentional element within a creative
representation.

Actors may represent individuals, collections, or procedurally generated
elements while exposing the same architectural behavior.

VizClick currently defines three Actor types.

---

## Individual Actor

Represents a single intentional element.

Examples include:

- person
- animal
- product
- vehicle
- building
- prop
- tree

Individual Actors describe one identifiable creative element.

---

## Collective Actor

Represents multiple similar elements that behave as a single creative
unit.

Collective Actors avoid unnecessary duplication while preserving creative
meaning.

Examples include:

- crowd
- audience
- flock
- herd
- school of fish
- army
- forest
- swarm
- field of flowers

A Collective Actor is not a container of thousands of Actors.

It represents the collective itself.

Typical properties include:

- population
- density
- distribution
- variation
- shared appearance
- shared behavior
- shared motion
- shared intention

Example:

Audience

Population: 12,000

Density: High

Attention: Stage

Emotion: Excitement

Motion: Rhythmic

---

## Procedural Actor

Represents creative elements generated through rules rather than explicit
instances.

Examples include:

- mountain range
- galaxy
- city skyline
- cloud layer
- star field
- terrain
- procedural vegetation
- particle systems

Procedural Actors describe generation rules rather than individual
objects.

Typical properties include:

- generation method
- scale
- variation
- randomness
- repetition
- distribution

---

# Responsibilities

An Actor encapsulates the complete creative representation of one
intentional element.

Actors are one of the primary Creative Briefs represented within the
Creative Representation Layer.

Each Actor is composed from reusable Creative Concepts stored within the
Creative Knowledge Ecology (CKE).

Actors reference Creative Concepts rather than embedding renderer-specific
descriptions.

Depending on the creative domain, an Actor may include:

- Concept
- Properties
- Appearance
- Performance
- Pose
- Motion
- Expression

Not every Actor requires every component.

For example:

- a person may include Performance and Pose
- a product may include Appearance
- a mountain may only include Appearance
- a flock may emphasize Motion
- a galaxy may describe procedural generation

This flexibility allows one architectural model to support every creative
domain.

---

# Relationships

Actors remain independent.

Meaning emerges through Relationships.

Relationships describe how Actors interact, influence one another, and
contribute to the overall creative representation.

Examples include:

- spatial relationships
- physical interactions
- emotional relationships
- narrative relationships
- visual composition
- temporal relationships
- symbolic relationships

Examples:

Singer

facing

Audience

Wolf Pack

surrounding

Deer

Watch

resting on

Marble Surface

Actors never own Relationships.

Relationships never own Actors.

Both are composed together within the Creative Representation Layer.

---

# Design Principles

## Architectural Abstraction

Actor is an architectural abstraction.

It intentionally ignores physical classifications in favor of creative
meaning.

An Actor represents the creative role performed within a creative
representation rather than the real-world identity of an element.

---

## Uniform Representation

All Actor types expose the same architectural behavior.

Regardless of whether an Actor represents a person, a crowd, or a
procedurally generated galaxy, every Actor participates in the Creative
Representation Layer through:

- Creative Concepts
- Relationships
- Creative Briefs

Renderer Adapters determine how each Actor type is realized for a
specific rendering system.

---

## Knowledge Driven

Actors are composed from reusable Creative Concepts defined within the
Creative Knowledge Ecology.

Creative Concepts remain immutable.

Actors compose those concepts into meaningful creative representations.

---

## Renderer Independence

Actors never contain:

- prompt syntax
- renderer parameters
- keyword weighting
- renderer optimizations

These belong exclusively to Renderer Adapters.

---

## Composability

Complex creative works emerge through combinations of Actors connected by
Relationships.

Meaning grows through composition rather than inheritance.

---

## Extensibility

New Actor types may be introduced without modifying the architecture.

Future examples may include:

- Volumetric Actor
- Simulation Actor
- Temporal Actor
- Composite Actor
- Digital Twin Actor

The Actor abstraction remains stable as VizClick evolves.

---

# Consequences

## Positive

- Unified representation across creative domains
- Renderer independence
- Reusable creative knowledge
- Flexible composition
- Scalable scene representation
- Efficient handling of large collections
- Support for procedural content
- Future-proof architecture

## Trade-offs

- Requires explicit Relationship modeling
- Introduces additional architectural abstractions
- Requires Creative Concept composition

These trade-offs are accepted because they produce a more expressive,
maintainable, and extensible representation of creative intent.

---

# Philosophy

Actors are not defined by what they are.

Actors are defined by the creative role they perform.

Creative Concepts provide reusable knowledge.

Actors compose that knowledge.

Relationships create meaning.

Renderer Adapters translate creative intent into renderer-specific
implementations.

Together they form a renderer-independent representation of creativity.

---

# Guiding Principle

> Every intentional element is represented as an Actor.
>
> An Actor may represent an individual, a collective, or a procedural
> construction, but it always represents creative intent rather than
> implementation.